  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/f/f9/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_115.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A haggard man is shown in India seeking \"the Ancient One,\" seeking a \"magic healing power.\"&nbsp; He encounters an old man who claims to be the one he's seeking but who also indicates that he bestows his power only on those <i>worthy</i> of it.&nbsp; After probing the traveler's mind, the Ancient One learns that he is Stephen Strange, a former famous surgeon who cared only about money, refusing to help any who could not afford his services, turning down every opportunity to give back to mankind unless he was compensated for his time.\n\nBut an auto accident changed all that in seconds.&nbsp; Though his life was spared, the accident caused irreparable damage to the nerves in his hands, rendering them shaky and unsuitable for surgery.&nbsp; It was a devastating blow and threatened to spell the end of his career.&nbsp; In bitterness, he spurned many offers to become the assistant of others, refusing to stoop so low as to work for another, even as a consultant.&nbsp; He withdrew from society, losing track of time, eventually becoming little more than a drifter.\n\nBut one day, while walking on a dock, he heard rumors of an \"Ancient One,\" whose power to cure was based on magic.&nbsp; With nothing to lose, Dr. Strange set out to find this \"Ancient One,\" which led him finally here, months later, to India... and into the presence of the Ancient One himself.\n\nBut the old man refuses to heal him, due to his selfish motives, unless he stays and studies with him.&nbsp; Only then might he find the cure he seeks.&nbsp; But to Strange, this only seems to confirm that the old man is a fraud, and he prepares to depart in anger and disappointment.\n\nBut the sudden arrival of a snowstorm prevents his departure, and he's forced to accept the hospitality of the old man, who offers him a room until the surrounding area thaws enough for travel.&nbsp; Entering the elaborate dwelling, he is shown to his room by another pupil, a \"creepy-looking character\" named Mordo.\n\nThe snow does not immediately melt, and Strange notes that Mordo does little else but study scrolls and recite dirges.&nbsp; When he ventures to the Ancient One's quarters to ask how long it takes for snow to melt here, he observes the old man asleep and suddenly attacked by the \"Vapors of Valtorr!\"&nbsp; Strange then witnesses the Ancient One fighting off a mystical attack, only then coming to believe that magic is real.&nbsp; The Ancient One admits he is constantly beset by the forces of evil and indicates that he's seeking a successor, as these forces can never be allowed to come to Earth.\n\nBut Strange does not truly believe until he happens to see Mordo offering an incense to someone named \"Dormammu.\"&nbsp; He realizes that the attack came from Mordo.&nbsp; The Ancient One's <i>own pupil</i> is trying to kill him!\n\nSeeing that Dr. Strange has spotted him, Mordo admits that he is <i>indeed</i> trying to slay his master, in order to become \"master of black magic.\"&nbsp; But since he believes he is more powerful than the \"unknowing Western dog,\" he casts a spell on Strange that prevents him from informing the Ancient One of anything that he's learned.&nbsp; Strange is blocked from saying anything and finally realizes that the magic here is real.\n\nHe tries to warn the Ancient One, but smug Mordo, standing right there, knows he cannot reveal the truth.&nbsp; But he can still attempt to learn magic from the Ancient One so that he can \"battle Mordo with his own weapons!\"\n\nThe Ancient One is pleased that Strange has finally seen the light and frees him from Mordo's spell, which of course he knew about, for \"the pupil can have no secrets from his master!\"&nbsp; Despite the constant threat, the Ancient One nevertheless prefers to keep Mordo close by, to more easily observe his plans and stop them.\n\nBut he tells Strange that \"one day, my son, when I am gone, it will be <i>your</i> task to battle Mordo... to the finish!\"\n\nDr. Strange then learns the ways of the mystic arts over the next weeks, months, and years.&nbsp; Finally, his life takes on a new meaning, and he stands ready for the epic battles ahead -- ready at last to battle Mordo when at last he strikes -- as \"Dr. Strange, master of black magic!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "The origin story (while now well-known due to the MCU movie), was still a solid and inventive one for its day.&nbsp; I continue to acknowledge a \"disinterest\" in the character, but I did at least read this tale with some bit of fascination, so I applaud it.&nbsp; Hopefully there will be more of the same in the months that follow.\n\n[ASSET: left | StrangeTheBum | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: right | WeakWesternDog | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | OfferAccepted | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nAs for the artwork, Steve Ditko's quirky style works for this character every bit as much as it does for Spider-Man, and I find that I actually like it, though I am hardly a Ditko fan.&nbsp; The rays that emanate like lightning bolts from the various characters' hands remind me of Spider-Man's early encounters with Electro, and the mists that always seem to be present around these mysterious figures seem fully in character.&nbsp; It's not a genre I gravitate to, but the surroundings for it seem well done.\n\n\nThus I award it a FN-, just missing out on a full mid-grade rating.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe splash page acknowledges that [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=whoops], this probably should have been done a few issues earlier.&nbsp; But ordering snafu doesn't change the fact that this was the best <i>Dr. Strange</i> outing thus far.\n\nThat being said, since this was clearly an important story, you'd think there might have been at least <i>some</i> notice of it on the cover, but there was <i>literally</i> no mention of Dr. Strange anywhere to be found.&nbsp; I <i>understand</i> that showing Sandman against the Human Torch was a draw, in and of itself, but... [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=nothing at all?]&nbsp; That's pretty harsh if you ask me!&nbsp; (I actually owned a copy of this book, and it wasn't until I actually opened it and paged through it that I realized what <i>else</i> was special about it.)",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}